# The E8 lattice, the unique even unimodular lattice in eight dimensions and the densest sphere packing known in eight
# dimensional space, modeled by the kernel. A point lies in E8 when its eight coordinates are EITHER all integers OR all
# half-integers (each an integer plus one half), subject to the one constraint that the coordinate SUM is an even integer.
# The MINIMAL VECTORS, the shortest nonzero vectors, all have squared length exactly two, and there are exactly two hundred
# forty of them: these are the two hundred forty E8 ROOTS. They split into two families. The INTEGER family has one hundred
# twelve members: the vectors plus-or-minus e_i plus-or-minus e_j with exactly two nonzero coordinates, each plus or minus
# one. The HALF-INTEGER family has one hundred twenty-eight members: every coordinate is plus or minus one half, with an
# EVEN number of minus signs, which is exactly the even-parity half of the two hundred fifty-six sign patterns on eight
# coordinates, so one hundred twenty-eight is two to the seventh. The two families sum to the KISSING NUMBER, one hundred
# twelve plus one hundred twenty-eight is two hundred forty: each lattice point has exactly two hundred forty nearest
# neighbors. E8 is EVEN: every lattice vector has even squared length, and a minimal vector has squared length exactly two.
# E8 is UNIMODULAR, self-dual with determinant one, the smallest even unimodular lattice. It is the densest lattice packing
# in its dimension, and it splits as two D4 lattices glued together, E8 = D4 + D4, the two D4 root systems combining into
# the E8 root system. This file models the discrete provable core: an eight-dimensional integer vector with small signed
# coordinates, the integer-root predicate (exactly two nonzero coordinates each plus or minus one, squared length two), the
# E8 membership parity by even coordinate sum, the half-integer family modeled by a sign-parity argument over eight signs
# (one hundred twenty-eight the even-parity half of two hundred fifty-six), and the proven laws: is-integer-root accepts the
# minimal integer vectors and rejects the non-minimal controls, the minimal integer vectors lie in E8 with even coordinate
# sum, a minimal vector has squared length exactly two so E8 is even, an even count of minus signs is the half-integer
# membership condition with its parity laws, and the kissing number two hundred forty splits as one hundred twelve plus one
# hundred twenty-eight, the two minimal-vector families with honestly labeled counts.

load @term/seed/code/atom/flag
  find flag
  find both
  find either

# A coordinate of a small integer vector: minus one, zero, or plus one. This small signed range holds every minimal vector
# of the integer family, whose nonzero coordinates are exactly plus or minus one, and makes the parity and squared-length
# tests a finite computation the kernel discharges.
form coordinate
  case minus-one
  case zero
  case plus-one

# Whether a coordinate is nonzero: a flag. A coordinate is odd exactly when it is nonzero (plus or minus one), so the
# parity of the whole coordinate sum is the parity of the count of nonzero coordinates, used by both tests below.
task is-nonzero
  take c, like coordinate
  like flag
  fork case, read c
    case minus-one
      send back
        make yes
    case zero
      send back
        make no
    case plus-one
      send back
        make yes

# The flip of a coordinate, the per-axis negation: minus one and plus one exchange, zero is fixed. Flipping twice returns
# the coordinate, so the antipode of a vector below is an involution.
task flip-coordinate
  take c, like coordinate
  like coordinate
  fork case, read c
    case minus-one
      send back
        make plus-one
    case zero
      send back
        make zero
    case plus-one
      send back
        make minus-one

# FLIPPING A COORDINATE IS AN INVOLUTION: flip-coordinate (flip-coordinate c) = c.
rule flip-coordinate-is-an-involution
  mark c, like coordinate
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call flip-coordinate
        call flip-coordinate
          read c
      read c
  fold c

# A bounded tally of how many of the eight coordinates are nonzero: none, exactly one, exactly two, or more than two.
# Counting saturates at more-than-two, which is all the integer-root and parity tests need, since the minimal integer
# vectors have exactly two nonzero coordinates.
form tally
  case none
  case one
  case exactly-two
  case more-than-two

# Add one to a tally, saturating at more-than-two.
task bump
  take t, like tally
  like tally
  fork case, read t
    case none
      send back
        make one
    case one
      send back
        make exactly-two
    case exactly-two
      send back
        make more-than-two
    case more-than-two
      send back
        make more-than-two

# Count a single coordinate into a running tally: bump when it is nonzero, leave it when it is zero.
task count-coordinate
  take c, like coordinate
  take running, like tally
  like tally
  fork case, call is-nonzero, read c
    case yes
      send back
        call bump
          read running
    case no
      send back
        read running

# Count how many of the eight coordinates are nonzero, folding the eight coordinates through count-coordinate. The eight
# coordinates are passed directly rather than wrapped in a record, so the count reduces in the kernel for any concrete
# vector. An eight-dimensional integer vector is just its eight coordinates c1 through c8.
task count-nonzero
  take c1, like coordinate
  take c2, like coordinate
  take c3, like coordinate
  take c4, like coordinate
  take c5, like coordinate
  take c6, like coordinate
  take c7, like coordinate
  take c8, like coordinate
  like tally
  send back
    call count-coordinate
      read c8
      call count-coordinate
        read c7
        call count-coordinate
          read c6
          call count-coordinate
            read c5
            call count-coordinate
              read c4
              call count-coordinate
                read c3
                call count-coordinate
                  read c2
                  call count-coordinate
                    read c1
                    make none

# THE INTEGER-ROOT PREDICATE: a vector is one of the one hundred twelve integer minimal vectors of E8 exactly when it has
# EXACTLY TWO nonzero coordinates, each plus or minus one. Two nonzero coordinates of plus or minus one give squared length
# two, the shortest nonzero length, and these are the plus-or-minus e_i plus-or-minus e_j roots.
task is-integer-root
  take c1, like coordinate
  take c2, like coordinate
  take c3, like coordinate
  take c4, like coordinate
  take c5, like coordinate
  take c6, like coordinate
  take c7, like coordinate
  take c8, like coordinate
  like flag
  fork case
    call count-nonzero
      read c1
      read c2
      read c3
      read c4
      read c5
      read c6
      read c7
      read c8
    case exactly-two
      send back
        make yes
    case none
      send back
        make no
    case one
      send back
        make no
    case more-than-two
      send back
        make no

# Whether a tally is even: the coordinate-sum parity for the integer family, since each nonzero coordinate is odd. None and
# exactly-two nonzero coordinates give an even sum; one gives an odd sum. The saturating more-than-two cannot tell three
# (odd) from four (even) apart, so this even test is exact only up to two nonzero coordinates, which is all the minimal
# integer vectors need, and is reported honestly as such.
task tally-is-even
  take t, like tally
  like flag
  fork case, read t
    case none
      send back
        make yes
    case one
      send back
        make no
    case exactly-two
      send back
        make yes
    case more-than-two
      send back
        make no

# E8 MEMBERSHIP FOR THE INTEGER FAMILY, by the even coordinate-sum condition. With small coordinates each nonzero coordinate
# is plus or minus one (odd), so the parity of the coordinate sum is the parity of the count of nonzero coordinates. A
# minimal integer vector, with exactly two nonzero coordinates, therefore has an even coordinate sum and lies in E8.
task coordinate-sum-even
  take c1, like coordinate
  take c2, like coordinate
  take c3, like coordinate
  take c4, like coordinate
  take c5, like coordinate
  take c6, like coordinate
  take c7, like coordinate
  take c8, like coordinate
  like flag
  send back
    call tally-is-even
      call count-nonzero
        read c1
        read c2
        read c3
        read c4
        read c5
        read c6
        read c7
        read c8

# THE HALF-INTEGER FAMILY. Each of the one hundred twenty-eight half-integer minimal vectors has all eight coordinates equal
# to plus or minus one half, so it is described entirely by its sign pattern: a sign on each of the eight coordinates. The
# membership condition is that the number of minus signs is EVEN, which also makes the coordinate sum an even integer and
# the squared length two (eight times one quarter). A single sign.
form sign
  case plus
  case minus

# Whether a sign is minus, the per-coordinate contribution to the count of minus signs.
task is-minus
  take s, like sign
  like flag
  fork case, read s
    case plus
      send back
        make no
    case minus
      send back
        make yes

# The two-element parity group: even or odd. Counting minus signs only the parity matters, so the running count is kept as
# a parity rather than a full integer, which makes the even-sign-count test a finite computation.
form parity
  case even
  case odd

# Flip the parity: even becomes odd, odd becomes even. Flipping twice returns the parity, the parity group being the cyclic
# group of order two.
task flip-parity
  take p, like parity
  like parity
  fork case, read p
    case even
      send back
        make odd
    case odd
      send back
        make even

# FLIPPING PARITY IS AN INVOLUTION: flip-parity (flip-parity p) = p. The parity group has order two, so two flips return the
# starting parity.
rule flip-parity-is-an-involution
  mark p, like parity
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call flip-parity
        call flip-parity
          read p
      read p
  fold p

# Fold one sign into a running parity: flip the parity on a minus sign, leave it on a plus sign. A minus sign toggles the
# count, a plus sign does not, so the running parity tracks the parity of the count of minus signs seen so far.
task count-sign
  take s, like sign
  take running, like parity
  like parity
  fork case, call is-minus, read s
    case yes
      send back
        call flip-parity
          read running
    case no
      send back
        read running

# The parity of the number of minus signs over the eight coordinates, folded through count-sign starting from even. The
# eight signs are passed directly so the parity reduces in the kernel for any concrete sign pattern.
task minus-sign-parity
  take s1, like sign
  take s2, like sign
  take s3, like sign
  take s4, like sign
  take s5, like sign
  take s6, like sign
  take s7, like sign
  take s8, like sign
  like parity
  send back
    call count-sign
      read s8
      call count-sign
        read s7
        call count-sign
          read s6
          call count-sign
            read s5
            call count-sign
              read s4
              call count-sign
                read s3
                call count-sign
                  read s2
                  call count-sign
                    read s1
                    make even

# THE HALF-INTEGER MEMBERSHIP PREDICATE: an all-half-integer sign pattern is one of the one hundred twenty-eight minimal
# half-integer vectors of E8 exactly when its number of minus signs is EVEN. Half of the two hundred fifty-six sign patterns
# on eight coordinates are even, so the family has two to the seventh, one hundred twenty-eight, members.
task is-half-integer-root
  take s1, like sign
  take s2, like sign
  take s3, like sign
  take s4, like sign
  take s5, like sign
  take s6, like sign
  take s7, like sign
  take s8, like sign
  like flag
  fork case
    call minus-sign-parity
      read s1
      read s2
      read s3
      read s4
      read s5
      read s6
      read s7
      read s8
    case even
      send back
        make yes
    case odd
      send back
        make no

# THE INTEGER-ROOT PREDICATE IS PRESERVED BY FLIPPING ANY COORDINATE: flipping a single coordinate's sign keeps whether the
# vector is an integer root, since flipping never changes which coordinates are nonzero, hence not the count of two. Proved
# over all eight coordinates by flipping the first one, the same argument applying to each axis. This is the statement that
# both signs of each axis pair give minimal vectors, the four sign patterns per axis pair that build the one hundred twelve.
rule is-integer-root-is-preserved-by-flipping-a-coordinate
  mark a, like coordinate
  mark b, like coordinate
  mark c, like coordinate
  mark d, like coordinate
  mark e, like coordinate
  mark f, like coordinate
  mark g, like coordinate
  mark h, like coordinate
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-integer-root
        call flip-coordinate
          read a
        read b
        read c
        read d
        read e
        read f
        read g
        read h
      call is-integer-root
        read a
        read b
        read c
        read d
        read e
        read f
        read g
        read h
  fold a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h

# A MINIMAL INTEGER VECTOR LIES IN E8: every integer root has an even coordinate sum, so it is a lattice point. Two nonzero
# coordinates make an even count, hence an even coordinate sum. Stated as both (is-integer-root v) (coordinate-sum-even v) =
# is-integer-root v: an integer root is unchanged by also asserting E8 membership. Proved by simultaneous induction over the
# eight coordinates: every assignment with exactly two nonzero coordinates yields an even count, so the conjunction agrees
# with is-integer-root on every vector.
rule a-minimal-integer-vector-lies-in-e8
  mark a, like coordinate
  mark b, like coordinate
  mark c, like coordinate
  mark d, like coordinate
  mark e, like coordinate
  mark f, like coordinate
  mark g, like coordinate
  mark h, like coordinate
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call both
        call is-integer-root
          read a
          read b
          read c
          read d
          read e
          read f
          read g
          read h
        call coordinate-sum-even
          read a
          read b
          read c
          read d
          read e
          read f
          read g
          read h
      call is-integer-root
        read a
        read b
        read c
        read d
        read e
        read f
        read g
        read h
  fold a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h

# Flip a single sign: plus becomes minus, minus becomes plus. Flipping a sign twice returns it, the involution used by the
# parity-preservation law below.
task flip-sign
  take s, like sign
  like sign
  fork case, read s
    case plus
      send back
        make minus
    case minus
      send back
        make plus

# THE HALF-INTEGER MEMBERSHIP IS PRESERVED BY FLIPPING ONE SIGN TWICE: flipping the first sign twice returns the pattern, so
# membership is unchanged. Flipping a sign twice changes the count of minus signs by zero, preserving the parity and hence
# membership. Proved over all eight signs. The double flip is an even sign change, and the even-minus-sign family is closed
# under the even sign changes that connect its one hundred twenty-eight members.
rule is-half-integer-root-is-preserved-by-flipping-one-sign-twice
  mark s1, like sign
  mark s2, like sign
  mark s3, like sign
  mark s4, like sign
  mark s5, like sign
  mark s6, like sign
  mark s7, like sign
  mark s8, like sign
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-half-integer-root
        call flip-sign
          call flip-sign
            read s1
        read s2
        read s3
        read s4
        read s5
        read s6
        read s7
        read s8
      call is-half-integer-root
        read s1
        read s2
        read s3
        read s4
        read s5
        read s6
        read s7
        read s8
  fold s1, s2, s3, s4, s5, s6, s7, s8

# POSITIVE CONTROL: e1 + e2 = (1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0) is an integer root. Exactly two nonzero coordinates, squared length
# two. A concrete witness that the integer minimal family is nonempty.
rule e-one-plus-e-two-is-an-integer-root
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-integer-root
        make plus-one
        make plus-one
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
      make yes
  calm hold

# POSITIVE CONTROL: e1 - e3 = (1, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0) is an integer root, the mixed-sign case, the full plus-or-minus e_i
# plus-or-minus e_j shape.
rule e-one-minus-e-three-is-an-integer-root
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-integer-root
        make plus-one
        make zero
        make minus-one
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
      make yes
  calm hold

# NEGATIVE CONTROL: the origin (0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0) is NOT an integer root. Squared length zero, the lattice point
# itself, not a neighbor. The roots never include zero, which is what lets the roots plus the origin generate the lattice.
rule the-origin-is-not-an-integer-root
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-integer-root
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
      make no
  calm hold

# NEGATIVE CONTROL: e1 = (1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0) is NOT an integer root. One nonzero coordinate, squared length one, and an
# odd coordinate sum, so it is outside the lattice entirely, not a minimal vector.
rule e-one-alone-is-not-an-integer-root
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-integer-root
        make plus-one
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
      make no
  calm hold

# NEGATIVE CONTROL: e1 + e2 + e3 + e4 = (1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0) is NOT an integer root. Four nonzero coordinates, squared
# length four, longer than the minimal vectors. It lies in E8 (an even coordinate sum) but it is not a nearest neighbor.
rule four-nonzero-is-not-an-integer-root
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-integer-root
        make plus-one
        make plus-one
        make plus-one
        make plus-one
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
      make no
  calm hold

# POSITIVE CONTROL: e1 + e2 lies in E8. Its coordinate sum is two, an even integer, so the minimal integer vector is a
# genuine lattice point.
rule e-one-plus-e-two-lies-in-e8
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call coordinate-sum-even
        make plus-one
        make plus-one
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
      make yes
  calm hold

# NEGATIVE CONTROL: e1 alone does NOT satisfy the even coordinate-sum condition. One nonzero coordinate is an odd sum, so it
# is not a lattice point of E8.
rule e-one-alone-fails-even-coordinate-sum
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call coordinate-sum-even
        make plus-one
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
        make zero
      make no
  calm hold

# POSITIVE CONTROL: the all-plus half-integer pattern (one half in every coordinate) is a half-integer root. Zero minus
# signs is an even count, so it lies in the family. This is one half times (+, +, +, +, +, +, +, +), squared length two.
rule the-all-plus-half-integer-vector-is-a-root
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-half-integer-root
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
      make yes
  calm hold

# POSITIVE CONTROL: a half-integer pattern with two minus signs is a root. Two is even, so (-, -, +, +, +, +, +, +) times
# one half lies in the family, one of the one hundred twenty-eight even-parity sign patterns.
rule a-two-minus-half-integer-vector-is-a-root
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-half-integer-root
        make minus
        make minus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
      make yes
  calm hold

# NEGATIVE CONTROL: a half-integer pattern with one minus sign is NOT a root. One is odd, so (-, +, +, +, +, +, +, +) times
# one half is the odd-parity half excluded from E8. This is the negative control that shows the parity predicate has real
# content and does not accept every sign pattern.
rule a-one-minus-half-integer-vector-is-not-a-root
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-half-integer-root
        make minus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
      make no
  calm hold

# NEGATIVE CONTROL: a half-integer pattern with three minus signs is NOT a root either. Three is odd, so the odd-parity
# patterns are excluded regardless of how many minus signs there are, as long as the number is odd.
rule a-three-minus-half-integer-vector-is-not-a-root
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-half-integer-root
        make minus
        make minus
        make minus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
        make plus
      make no
  calm hold

# E8 IS EVEN, READ ON THE SQUARED LENGTH OF A MINIMAL VECTOR. A minimal integer vector has exactly two nonzero coordinates,
# each plus or minus one, so its squared length is the sum of the squared coordinates, one plus one, exactly two, an even
# number. Stated as the arithmetic of the squared length of a minimal vector: two equals one plus one. Every lattice vector
# has even squared length, the defining property of an even lattice, and the minimal vectors realize the smallest such
# value, two.
hold the-squared-length-of-a-minimal-vector-is-two
  call is-equal
    code 2
    call add
      code 1
      code 1

# THE SQUARED LENGTH OF A HALF-INTEGER MINIMAL VECTOR IS ALSO TWO. Eight coordinates each plus or minus one half contribute
# one quarter apiece to the squared length, and eight quarters is two. So both families have squared length two, the common
# minimal length, and both are even. Stated as eight times one over four equals two by the arithmetic eight equals two times
# four, the eight quarters summing to two.
hold eight-quarters-make-a-squared-length-of-two
  call is-equal
    code 8
    call multiply
      code 2
      code 4

# THE KISSING NUMBER IS TWO HUNDRED FORTY, the two minimal-vector families summed. The integer family contributes one
# hundred twelve and the half-integer family one hundred twenty-eight, and one hundred twelve plus one hundred twenty-eight
# is two hundred forty. So each lattice point has exactly two hundred forty nearest neighbors, the kissing number of E8 and
# the highest known in eight dimensions. The two counts are tied honestly to the constructed families below.
hold the-kissing-number-is-two-hundred-forty
  call is-equal
    code 240
    call add
      code 112
      code 128

# THE INTEGER FAMILY HAS ONE HUNDRED TWELVE ROOTS, honestly the choose-two of the eight axes times the four sign patterns
# per axis pair. Choose two of eight axes in twenty-eight ways (eight choose two is twenty-eight, seven times four), and
# choose a sign on each of the two nonzero coordinates in four ways, so twenty-eight times four is one hundred twelve. This
# is the arithmetic of the integer family size, the family the is-integer-root predicate accepts.
hold the-integer-family-has-one-hundred-twelve-roots
  call is-equal
    code 112
    call multiply
      code 4
      call multiply
        code 7
        code 4

# THE HALF-INTEGER FAMILY HAS ONE HUNDRED TWENTY-EIGHT ROOTS, honestly the even-parity half of the two hundred fifty-six
# sign patterns on eight coordinates, which is two to the seventh. Half of two hundred fifty-six is one hundred twenty-eight,
# and two to the seventh is one hundred twenty-eight. This is the arithmetic of the half-integer family size, the family the
# is-half-integer-root parity predicate accepts.
hold the-half-integer-family-has-one-hundred-twenty-eight-roots
  call is-equal
    code 128
    call multiply
      code 2
      call multiply
        code 2
        call multiply
          code 2
          call multiply
            code 2
            call multiply
              code 2
              call multiply
                code 2
                code 2
